    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>

<p>We construct a life table as follows:</p>
<p>

<ol>
<li>
<p>To calculate the values of \( l_x \) (the number of survivors to exact age \( x \)), we can follow these steps:</p>
<ul>
<li>For \( x = 0 \) (which corresponds to newborns at week \(0\)), let \( l_0 = {@l0@} \) newborn babies initially.</li>
<li>For each subsequent week (\( x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 \)), we'll use the given survival rates to calculate the number of survivors.</li><br>
</ul>
<p>For \( x = {@weeks[1]@} \) (week \(0\)): </p>
<p>\( l_0 = {@lx[1]@} \) (initial number of newborns)</p><br>

<p>For \( x = {@weeks[2]@} \) (end of week \(1\)): </p>
<p>\( \displaystyle  l_1 = l_0 - \frac{{@p1@}}{100} \times l_0 = {@lx[1]@} -{@float(p1/100)@} \times {@lx[1]@} = {@lx[2]@} \)</p><br>

<p>For \( x = {@weeks[3]@}  \) (end of week \(2\)): </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle  l_2 = l_1 - \frac{{@p2@}}{100} \times l_1 = {@lx[2]@} - {@float(p2/100)@} \times {@lx[2]@} = {@lx[3]@} \)</p><br>

<p>For \( x = {@weeks[4]@}  \) (end of week \(3\)): </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle  l_3 = l_2 - \frac{{@p3@}}{100} \times l_2 = {@lx[3]@} - {@float(p3/100)@} \times {@lx[3]@} = {@lx[4]@}\)</p>
<p>\( l_3 = {@lx[4]@} \) (rounded to nearest whole number)</p><br>

<p>For \( x = {@weeks[5]@}  \) (end of week \(4\)): </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle  l_4 = l_3 - \frac{{@p4@}}{100} \times l_3 = {@lx[4]@} - {@float(p4/100)@} \times {@lx[4]@} = {@lx[5]@} \)</p><br>

<p>For \( x = {@weeks[6]@}  \) (end of week \(5\)): </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle  l_5 = l_4 - \frac{{@p5@}}{100} \times l_4 = {@lx[5]@} - {@float(p5/100)@} \times {@lx[5]@} = {@lx[6]@} \)</p><br>

<p>For \( x = {@weeks[7]@}  \) (end of week \(6\)): </p>
<p>\( l_6 = 0 \) (All babies have left the hospital)</p><br>
</li>

<li><p>Given the formula:</p>
<p>\({}_\text{n}\text{d}_\text{x}  = l_x – l_{x+n}\) (this is the number of deaths between ages \(x\) and \(x+n\))</p>
<p>For \( x = {@weeks[1]@}  \): </p>
<p>We have,</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle {@lx_values[1]@}-{@lx_n_values[1]@}={@ndx_values[1]@}\)</p>
<p>Use the same procedure to compute \({}_\text{n}\text{d}_\text{x}\)  for \(x=1,2,3,4,5,6\)</p><br>
</li>

<li><p>Given the formula:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle   {}_\text{n}\text{q}_\text{x}  =\frac{{}_\text{n}\text{d}_\text{x} }{l_x} =\frac{l_x – l_{x+n}}{l_x}\) (this is the probability of an individual alive at age \(x\) dying in the age interval \(x – (x+n)\))</p>
<p>For \( x = {@weeks[1]@}  \): </p><p>We have,</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \frac{{@ndx_values[1]@}}{{@lx_values[1]@}}={@nqx_values[1]@}\)</p>
<p>Use the same procedure to compute \({}_\text{n}\text{q}_\text{x}\)  for \(x=1,2,3,4,5,6\)</p><br>
</li>


<li><p>Given the formula:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle L_x = \frac{(l_x + l_{x+1})}{2}\) This is the number of persons now alive and aged between \(x\) and \(x+1\) exactly (also called survivors between \(x\) and \(x+1\))</p>
<p>For \( x = {@weeks[1]@}  \): </p><p>We have,</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle \frac{{@lx_values[1]@}+{@lx_n_values[1]@}}{{@2@}}={@midpoint_values_Lx[1]@}\)</p>
<p>Use the same procedure to compute \(\text{L}_\text{x}\)  for \(x=1,2,3,4,5,6\)</p><br>
</li>

<li><p>Given the formula:</p>
<p>\(\text{T}_x=\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} \text{L}_{x+i}\) This is the total number of person-years lived between ages \(x\) and \(x+n\) </p>
<p>We have,the \(  T_x = L_x + L_{x+1} + L_{x+2} +  \ldots +  L_{x+n}\)</p>
<p>We obtain the cumulative sum of: </p>
<p>\(\displaystyle {@midpoint_values_Lxr@}={@Tx@}\)  for \(x=1,2,3,4,5,6\)</p>
<p>So, \(T_x\) can also mean the life table total population</p><br>
</li>
<li><p>Given the formula:</p>
<p>\(\displaystyle e_{x}^{0}=\frac{\text{this is the total number of years lived after age} \, x}{\text{these are the people who are going to live the years}}=\frac{T_x}{l_x}\)</p>
<p>We have,</p>
<p>For \( x = {@weeks[1]@}  \): </p><p>\(\displaystyle \frac{{@Tx_values[1]@}}{{@lx_values[1]@}}={@e0x_values[1]@}\)</p>
<p>Use the same procedure to compute \(\text{L}_\text{x}\)  for \(x=1,2,3,4,5,6\)</p><br>
</li>
</ol>
<ol class="HELM_parts">
