</li>

<p>Using the information in the table above:</p>
      <li><p>How many cots are needed? (Number of beds needed to accommodate newborn babies) </p>
<p>If there are {@newborn_per_week@} newborn babies admitted each week, then the stationary population resulting will be;</p>
<p> \( \begin{align} \displaystyle 
\frac{{@newborn_per_week@} }{l_0} \times T_0 &= \frac{{@newborn_per_week@} }{{@l0@}} \times {@total_Lx@} \\
                     &= {@max_cots_at_hospital@} \\
\\

\text{Also obtained by recalling that:} \\
\frac{l_0}{T_0} \times \text{Required} &= \text{Maintained value} \\
l_0 \times \frac{R}{{@total_Lx@}} &={@newborn_per_week@} \\
         R &= \frac{{@newborn_per_week@}}{l_0} \times {@total_Lx@} \\
            &=   \frac{{@newborn_per_week@}}{{@l0@}} \times {@total_Lx@} \\
            &= {@max_cots_at_hospital@} \\
\text{So},\,  {@max_cots_at_hospital@}  \, \text{cots or beds are required to accommodate newborn babies}
\end{align}\)</p>
</li>
      <li><p>At any one time, determine the average stay in the hospital.</p>
<p>The average stay in the hospital is given by;</p>
<p> \( \begin{align} \displaystyle 
e_x^0 &=\frac{T_0}{l_0} \\
          &= \frac{ {@total_Lx@}}{{@l0@}} \\
          &= {@average_stay@} \, \text{weeks}
 \end{align}\)</p>
</li>
        </ol>]]></text>
